1	/**
2	 * notify.ts — Resolve who to notify (a GitHub login) for a given issue or
3	 * PR. The one canonical implementation, used directly (no more subprocess
4	 * spawn) by every caller: mcp/github.ts, concludeIssue,
5	 * the aggregation gate (lib/aggregation.ts), and
6	 * src/entrypoints/machinery/resolve_notify.ts's thin CLI wrapper (kept because
7	 * atomaton-runner.wac.ts invokes it as a workflow step, via `scriptCommand`).
8	 *
9	 * # This is the fallback, not the rule
10	 *
11	 * Who gets notified is decided where a run starts, and every live path already
12	 * answers the question this module answers badly. A run started by a comment
13	 * notifies whoever typed it (`comment.user.login`); a run started by an issue
14	 * notifies whoever opened it (`sender.login`); a run one agent hands to another
15	 * carries the same login onward, so a chain notifies whoever began it. That is the
16	 * rule -- **the person who asked for this run**, not the person who owns the thread.
17	 *
18	 * This module exists for when that plumbing delivers nothing: a caller that forgot
19	 * to pass it, a dispatch path added later, a body whose tag was lost. The runner
20	 * calls it only when `inputs.notify` arrives empty. Read what follows as recovery,
21	 * and do not extend it as though it were the policy -- if a new trigger needs a
22	 * notify, it decides one at the trigger, where it knows who acted.
23	 *
24	 * # What recovery does
25	 *
26	 * Looks for an `<!-- atomaton:notify=LOGIN -->` tag in the body -- embedded by
27	 * mcp/github.ts at creation time, carrying the requester the creating run knew.
28	 *
29	 * Falls back to the issue/PR's own author when no tag is present and the author is
30	 * a human. If neither is available, walks up one edge -- GitHub's own sub-issue link
31	 * for an issue, the `atomaton:parent-issue` tag for a pull request -- and retries on
32	 * the parent. Measured over all 255 issues in this repository,
33	 * no issue would need that walk: every one resolves by its own tag or its own
34	 * author. It is kept for a repository whose history is not this one.
35	 *
36	 * Last, the repository owner, so that a failure reaches somebody rather than
37	 * nobody. That is a weaker claim than the others -- the owner did not ask for this
38	 * run and may not know what it was -- so the comment that mentions them says why
39	 * they are being told.
40	 *
41	 * Never throws for missing data -- callers treat an empty result as
42	 * "nobody to notify".
